INT. COACHING INN - COMMON ROOM - NIGHT - 1817

A low room, black beams, a hearth coughing smoke. Rain beats the shutters. A single tallow candle gutters on a long table scarred with a century of knives.

INNKEEPER SILAS PRYCE (50s, bald, apron stiff with grease) wipes the same tankard he has wiped for an hour. He is a man who has learned to be furniture.

At the far end, a WOMAN IN A DRIPPING TRAVELLING CLOAK (30s, pale, gloves the colour of wet slate) sits very upright, her hands folded. She has not removed her bonnet. Rain runs from its ribbons onto her collar.

By the fire, a YOUNG SOLDIER (20s, red coat unbuttoned, one boot off) toasts his stockinged foot and does not look at either of them.

SILAS
(to the room)
Last coach was due at nine. Roads are drowned between here and Dunstable. Nobody leaves tonight.

The soldier grunts. The woman does not move.

SILAS (CONT'D)
(to the woman)
Ma'am, will you be wanting a room?

STRANGER
I will want to sit.

SILAS
Sit as long as you please, then.

He goes back to the tankard. The fire pops. A log collapses.

YOUNG SOLDIER
(muttering, to the flames)
God rot this country. God rot these roads.

The stranger turns her head, slowly, toward him. Studies the unbuttoned coat. The bare foot.

STRANGER
You are far from your regiment, sir.

YOUNG SOLDIER
(not turning)
I'm where I'm sent.

STRANGER
By whom?

He does not answer. His heel rubs the hearthstone, up and down.

SILAS
Leave the lad be, ma'am. He's had a hard road.

STRANGER
We all have. It is why we are here.

She rises. The chair legs scrape. She crosses the room at a measured pace and stops by the table, close enough to the candle that her face is lit from below for the first time: a long jaw, grey eyes, a scar that bisects one eyebrow.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
Landlord. How many guests do you keep?

SILAS
(guarded)
Three tonight, now. Yourself, the lad, and me.

STRANGER
Four, I think. Counting the one in the cellar.

Silas's tankard stops. The cloth hangs in his hand.

SILAS
There's none in my cellar.

YOUNG SOLDIER
(finally turning)
Captain Hale.

The stranger looks at him. Something in her face releases, like a hand opening.

HALE
You know me, then.

YOUNG SOLDIER
Everyone knows you. They said you'd hanged.

HALE
They said that of my husband.

Silas sets the tankard down at last.

SILAS
(to himself, barely audible)
Lord. Lord, forgive an old fool.

HALE
(to the whole room)
Nobody will be hurt. I have come for what is in the cellar, and then I shall go back into the rain.

The soldier shakes his head. He reaches for the boot he has taken off, and for the sword beside it.

YOUNG SOLDIER
I can't let you.

HALE
No. I know. That is why I wore the gloves.

She raises her hands. The wet slate gloves are stitched, at the knuckles, with something small and bright.

The candle flares. The shutters bang open on the storm.

SMASH CUT TO BLACK.
